.TH sed "" "Command"
.SH NAME
sed \- stream editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBsed [\-n] [\-e \fIcommands\fB] [\-f \fIscript\fB] ... \fIfile ...\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
sed -- stream editor
Usage:	sed [ -n ] [ -e commands ] [ -f script ] ... file ...
Options:
	-e	Direct commands follow
	-f	File name of command script follows
	-n	No implicit output
.HE
.B sed
copies each
.IR file ,
or the standard input if none is given, to the standard output,
applying to it the editing commands given by
.B \-e
arguments and
.B \-f
script files, in the order those arguments appear on the command
line.
If neither
.B \-e
nor
.B \-f
is given, the first non-option argument is taken as a single
.B \-e
command instead of a file name.
.PP
.B sed
reads one line at a time into a
.IR "pattern space" ,
runs every command whose address selects that line against it, and
then, unless
.B \-n
was given, writes the pattern space to the standard output before
reading the next line.
There is also a
.I "hold space"
that commands may copy the pattern space into and out of.
.SH ADDRESSES
A command may be preceded by zero, one, or two
.I addresses.
With no address, the command applies to every line.
With one address, it applies only to a line selected by that address.
With two addresses, separated by a comma, it applies to the first
line selected by the first address, and to every following line up
to and including the next line selected by the second address; a new
such range cannot begin until the current one has ended.
An address is one of:
.IP \fIn\fR 0.75i
Line number
.I n
of the input (lines are numbered from 1 across all the files given).
.IP \fB$\fR
The last line of input.
.IP \fB/\fIpattern\fB/\fR
The next line that matches the regular expression
.IR pattern .
.PP
A
.B !
immediately before the command applies it to every line
.I not
selected by the address (or addresses).
.SH COMMANDS
Each command occupies one line (except that the
.BR a ,
.BR i ,
and
.B c
commands take following text lines, each but the last ending in
.BR \e ,
as their argument, and the
.BR s ,
.BR y ,
.BR w ,
and
.B r
commands take the rest of their line as argument).
Commands may be grouped in braces:
\fB[\fIaddress\fB[\fI,address\fB]]{\fRcommand ...\fB}\fR
runs each enclosed command on the selected lines.
.PP
.IP "\fB=\fR" 0.75i
Print the current line number.
.IP "\fBa\e\fR"
Append the following text (up to the line not ending in
.BR \e )
to the output after the current line.
.IP "\fBb \fI[label]\fR"
Branch unconditionally to
.IR label ,
or to the end of the script if
.I label
is omitted.
.IP "\fBc\e\fR"
Delete the pattern space (like
.BR d ),
and, once at the end of the addressed range, write the following text
in its place.
.IP "\fBd\fR"
Delete the pattern space and start the next cycle without printing it.
.IP "\fBD\fR"
Delete up to and including the first embedded newline in the pattern
space (left there by
.BR N ),
and start the next cycle on what remains, without reading new input;
if there is no embedded newline,
.B D
acts like
.BR d .
.IP "\fBg\fR"
Replace the pattern space with a copy of the hold space.
.IP "\fBG\fR"
Append a newline and a copy of the hold space to the pattern space.
.IP "\fBh\fR"
Replace the hold space with a copy of the pattern space.
.IP "\fBH\fR"
Append a newline and a copy of the pattern space to the hold space.
.IP "\fBi\e\fR"
Write the following text to the output before the current line.
.IP "\fBl\fR"
Write the pattern space to the output with non-graphic characters
made visible: backslash is doubled, and other non-graphic bytes are
shown as a backslash followed by a three-digit octal number; long
lines are folded, with a trailing backslash marking the fold.
.IP "\fBn\fR"
Write the pattern space to the output (unless
.B \-n
was given), then replace it with the next input line; if there is no
next line,
.B sed
exits.
.IP "\fBN\fR"
Append the next input line to the pattern space, separated by an
embedded newline; if there is no next line,
.B sed
exits without writing the pattern space.
.IP "\fBp\fR"
Write the pattern space to the output.
.IP "\fBP\fR"
Write up to the first embedded newline of the pattern space to the
output.
.IP "\fBq\fR"
Write the pattern space to the output (unless
.BR \-n ),
and exit immediately.
.IP "\fBr \fIfile\fR"
Read
.I file
and copy its contents to the output after the current line.
.IP "\fBs/\fIpattern\fB/\fIreplacement\fB/\fI[n][g][p][w file]\fR"
Search the pattern space for
.I pattern
and replace it with
.IR replacement .
By default only the first match is replaced; a digit
.I n
selects the
.IR n th
match instead, and
.B g
replaces every match from there on.
.B p
writes the pattern space if a replacement was made, and
.B w \fIfile\fR
appends it to
.I file
under the same condition.
In
.IR replacement ,
.B &
stands for the text that
.I pattern
matched, and
.BI \e d
(a digit
.IR d )
stands for the text delimited by the
.IR d th
.BR \e( ... \e) .
.IP "\fBt \fI[label]\fR"
Branch to
.I label
(or to the end of the script) if any substitution has succeeded since
the last input line was read or the last
.B t
branched.
.IP "\fBw \fIfile\fR"
Append the pattern space, followed by a newline, to
.IR file .
.IP "\fBx\fR"
Exchange the pattern space and the hold space.
.IP "\fBy/\fIchars\fB/\fIreplacements\fB/\fR"
Transliterate each character in the pattern space that appears in
.I chars
to the corresponding character in
.IR replacements ;
the two lists must be the same length.
.IP "\fB:\fIlabel\fR"
Declare
.I label
as the target of a later
.B b
or
.B t
command.
.SH "REGULAR EXPRESSIONS"
The patterns used in addresses and in the
.B s
command are regular expressions built from:
.IP \fBc\fR 0.75i
An ordinary character matches itself.
.IP \fB.\fR
Matches any single character.
.IP \fB*\fR
Matches zero or more occurrences of the single preceding character (or
of a preceding
.BR . ,
.BR [...] ,
or
.BR \e(...\e) ).
.IP \fB^\fR
At the start of a pattern, anchors the match to the beginning of the
pattern space.
.IP \fB$\fR
At the end of a pattern, anchors the match to the end of the pattern
space.
.IP "\fB[\fIchars\fB]\fR, \fB[^\fIchars\fB]\fR"
Matches any one character in (or, with a leading
.BR ^ ,
not in)
.IR chars ,
which may include ranges written
.IR c1 \- c2 .
.IP "\fB\e(\fR ... \fB\e)\fR"
Groups a sub-pattern so it can be referred to as
.BI \e d
(where
.I d
is 1-9), either later in the same pattern or, in a substitution's
replacement, as the text that sub-pattern matched.
Up to nine such groups are allowed in one pattern.
.IP \fB\en\fR
Matches a newline embedded in the pattern space (as left there by
.BR N ).
.IP \fB\ec\fR
Matches the literal character
.I c
even if it would otherwise be special.
.PP
If
.B \-s
is in effect, every letter typed in a pattern (whether alone, in a
character class, or as the range endpoints of a character class)
matches either its upper- or lower-case form in the pattern space.
.SH OPTIONS
.IP \fB\-e\fR
The next argument is one or more
.B sed
commands, separated by newlines.
More than one
.B \-e
(and
.BR \-f )
argument may be given; the commands are run in the order given.
.IP \fB\-f\fR
The next argument names a file of
.B sed
commands, one command per line.
.IP \fB\-n\fR
Suppress the automatic writing of the pattern space at the end of
each cycle; only explicit
.BR p ,
.BR P ,
.BR l ,
and similar output commands produce output.
.IP \fB\-s\fR
Fold case: a letter in a pattern matches either its upper- or
lower-case form in the input.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B sed
reports each error found while compiling the commands, with the
number of the command line it occurred on, and exits without
processing any input if there were any.
Execution-time errors (such as a buffer overflow) are also reported,
with a diagnostic and no line number.
.SH NOTES
The line, pattern, and hold buffers are each limited to 512
characters; a label name or a file name given to
.B w
or
.B r
is truncated to 15 characters.
Up to nine
.B \e(
\&...
.B \e)
groups are allowed per pattern.
